    "body": "Great on any Friday: Ecoflow River 3 Plus - Portable Power Station, 286Wh LiFePO4 Battery\nInsider Confirms That the 8.0-Liter Power Stroke Diesel Is Coming and Ford Has a Name for It\n7 photos\nPhoto: Ford\nThe rumor mill has been spinning for months. Now, here comes the confirmation. An insider says the speculation has a real basis. While Ford hasn't made an official announcement, the 8.0-liter Power Stroke turbo-diesel is reportedly coming, and Ford has come up with an internal code name for it.\nFor the 2027 model year, Ford reduced its heavy-duty Super Duty engine lineup for the F-250, F-350, and F-450 down to just two V8 powerplants: the 7.3-liter Godzilla gas V8 and the 6.7-liter High-Output Power Stroke turbo-diesel V8. Ford dropped its previous entry-level 6.8-liter gas V8 and the Standard-Output 6.7-liter diesel.\nThe 6.7-liter Scorpion High-Output Power Stroke produces 500 horsepower (507 metric horsepower) and a massive 1,200 pound-feet (1,627 Newton meters) of torque, while the 7.3-liter Godzilla makes do with 420 horsepower (426 metric horsepower) and 445 pound-feet (603 Newton meters).\nNow, it seems that Ford has another engine in the making. The automaker has reportedly sent it into the testing phase, an insider confirmed for The Fast Lane Truck YouTube channel. The insider also revealed the name of the engine. Ford is referring to it as the \"Roadrunner.\"\nPhoto: Ford Unofficial reports claim that it will come with between 550 and 650 horsepower (between 558 and 659 metric horsepower) and roughly 1,400 - 1,500 pound-feet (1,898 - 2,034 Newton meters) of torque, which will position it at the top of the lineup. A twin-turbo setup designed to build boost quickly at low rpm, while sustaining airflow under heavy towing loads, should be part of the plan.\nThis comes after earlier this year, TFL Truck dismissed the 8.0-liter V8 rumors as unverified internet speculation because there were no patents, no official Ford comments, and no mention of the engine in the 2027 Super Duty order guide. What the reliable source told them is now reversing their skepticism.\nThe Roadrunner will be an 8.0-liter Power Stroke turbo-diesel, with an engine capacity that will place it at the top of the range. But since the 2027 Super Duty lineup is already locked in, an 8.0-liter Roadrunner would most likely debut with the 2028 or even 2029 model year, alongside a major truck lineup redesign.\nThe Roadrunner is reportedly in active bench and prototype testing. Ford could offer the 8.0-liter as a heavy-towing upgrade above the 6.7-liter High-Output, similar to how it previously sold both the Standard-Output and the High-Output diesels, or possibly use it to replace the 6.7-liter entirely to meet stricter emissions standards. Up until then, the 6.7-liter High-Output Power Stroke remains Ford's sole heavy-duty diesel option.\nPhoto: Ford However, until Ford files public patents, releases the official specs, or lists the engine in a future order guide, the 8.0-liter remains a rumor rather than a guaranteed production engine. The move does make sense.\nFord would thus respond to competitors that scaled up their engine displacement. General Motors is reportedly developing an 8.3-liter Duramax (Mongoose), while Ram is rumored to have a 7.2-liter Cummins in the works.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nFord Ford V8 Ford Super Duty Ford Roadrunner ford trucks\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT 5 Spectacular Coachbuilt Wagon Versions of Famous GTs",
